# tidyfeed

`tidyfeed` is a small, dependency-free Node.js library that parses RSS 2.0 and Atom 1.0 feeds into a single normalized object shape, so you don't have to branch on feed format in your own code. It exposes one function, `parseFeed(xmlString)`, which returns `{ title, link, items: [{ id, title, link, published, summary }] }`, where `published` is an ISO 8601 string or `null`. It performs no network requests — you bring the XML, it brings the parsing. Note that it is not a streaming parser: the entire document is held in memory, and RSS enclosures and media extensions are not currently supported.

## Install

```sh
npm install tidyfeed
```

Requires Node.js 18 or later. No dependencies.

## Usage

```js
import { parseFeed } from 'tidyfeed';

const xml = await fetch('https://example.com/feed.xml').then((r) => r.text());
const feed = parseFeed(xml);

console.log(feed.title);
for (const item of feed.items) {
  console.log(item.title, item.link, item.published);
}
```

