# tidyfeed

tidyfeed is a dependency-free Node.js library (Node 18+) that parses RSS 2.0 and Atom 1.0 feeds into a single normalized object shape, so your code doesn't need to care which format a feed uses. It takes an XML string and returns the feed's title and link along with a list of items, each with an `id`, `title`, `link`, `published` date (an ISO 8601 string, or `null` if absent), and `summary`. tidyfeed does not fetch feeds over the network; you supply the XML. It is not a streaming parser, so the whole document is held in memory. RSS enclosures and media extensions are not currently supported.

## Install

```sh
npm install tidyfeed
```

## Usage

```js
import { parseFeed } from 'tidyfeed';

const xml = await (await fetch('https://example.com/feed.xml')).text();
const feed = parseFeed(xml);

console.log(feed.title);
for (const item of feed.items) {
  console.log(item.published, item.title, item.link);
}
```

`parseFeed` returns:

```js
{
  title,   // string
  link,    // string
  items: [
    { id, title, link, published, summary } // published: ISO 8601 string or null
  ]
}
```

## License

MIT

